@Francis
My experience in Band (never "the" band) was not typical. I signed up and was handed a bass clarinet, which I assumed someone would teach me how to play, and I was too shy and embarrassed to ask  This never happened, so at practice, at first period, where it was held in a freezing, unheated, former auto repair garage (no room in the school building), I sat there in my coat, my lipstick wrecking the mouthpiece reed, and prayed no one would notice that no sound ever came out of the instrument. I got away with it until one day,the band was playing a sort of doodledoodledo, clearly background music for something, and everyone was looking at me. After practice, Douglas, a master of the clarinet, confronted me: "I knew you couldn't play that thing, that was supposed to be your solo."  

At the end of the semester, I had a dreaded conference with the taciturn bandmaster, who seemed to hate us all. I don't remember the exact words, but the outcome was that I got a B, with the unspoken agreement that I never darkened his door again. My recollection  is that he was very kind about all of it, but there was no question about my ever presuming to take Band again.

